  "prompt": "Find the common rule that maps an input grid to an output grid, given the examples below. Generate a python script that takes a grid as input and transforms it according to the this rule. Anticipate that this program will be applied to several grids, not only the shown test grid.\n\nYour script must define exactly one callable entrypoint:\ndef solve(grid):\nwhere `grid` is passed as a list of lists of integers (list[list[int]]) and the function returns the transformed grid in the same format.\n\nExample 1:\n\nInput:\n3 6 6 6 7 8 8 8\n3 6 3 3 7 8 7 7\n3 3 3 6 7 8 7 8\n6 3 6 6 8 7 8 8\n7 7 7 7 4 4 4 4\n8 8 8 7 1 1 1 4\n7 7 8 8 4 4 4 1\n8 8 7 7 1 1 4 1\n4 1 4 4 3 6 3 3\n4 1 1 1 3 6 6 6\n4 4 1 4 3 3 6 3\n4 1 4 4 3 6 3 6\nOutput:\n3 6 6 6 7 8 8 8\n3 6 3 3 7 8 7 7\n3 3 3 6 7 7 7 8\n6 3 6 6 8 7 8 8\n7 7 7 7 4 4 4 4\n8 8 8 7 1 1 1 4\n7 7 8 8 4 4 1 1\n8 8 7 7 1 1 4 4\n4 1 4 4 3 6 3 3\n4 1 1 1 3 6 6 6\n4 4 1 4 3 3 6 3\n4 1 4 4 3 6 3 3\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput:\n5 2 2 5 2 4 8 8 4 8\n5 2 5 5 2 4 8 4 4 8\n5 5 2 2 5 8 4 8 8 4\n2 2 5 5 2 8 8 4 4 4\n2 5 5 2 2 8 4 4 8 8\n8 8 8 8 8 2 2 2 2 2\n8 4 4 4 8 2 5 5 5 2\n8 8 4 8 8 2 2 5 2 5\n4 8 8 4 4 5 2 2 2 5\n8 8 4 8 8 2 2 5 2 2\nOutput:\n5 2 2 5 2 4 8 8 4 8\n5 2 5 5 2 4 8 4 4 8\n5 5 2 2 5 4 4 8 8 4\n2 2 5 5 2 8 8 4 4 8\n2 5 5 2 2 8 4 4 8 8\n8 8 8 8 8 2 2 2 2 2\n8 4 4 4 8 2 5 5 5 2\n8 8 4 8 8 2 2 5 2 2\n4 8 8 4 4 5 2 2 5 5\n8 8 4 8 8 2 2 5 2 2\n\nBelow is a test input grid.\n\nInput:\n9 9 1 1 7 7 3 7\n9 1 9 9 7 3 7 3\n9 1 9 1 7 3 7 3\n1 9 1 1 3 7 3 3\n1 9 9 9 3 7 7 7\n3 3 7 7 9 1 1 1\n7 3 7 3 1 9 1 9\n7 7 7 3 1 9 1 9\n3 7 3 7 9 1 9 1\n7 3 7 7 1 9 1 1\n\nYour final answer should just be the python script itself, no other text or markdown.",
  "raw_response": "Looking at the examples, I notice that for each row, the right half should mirror the abstract binary pattern of the left half (with colors substituted). When they don't match, the right half is corrected to match.\n\nFor example, in Example 1 row 2: left `3 3 3 6` has pattern `A A A B`, but right `7 8 7 8` has pattern `A B A B`. It's corrected to `7 7 7 8` (pattern `A A A B`).\n\nThe color mapping between halves is determined by whichever of the two possible mappings produces the most matches.\n\n```python\ndef solve(grid):\n    rows = len(grid)\n    cols = len(grid[0])\n    half = cols // 2\n    result = [row[:] for row in grid]\n    \n    for r in range(rows):\n        left = grid[r][:half]\n        right = grid[r][half:]\n        \n        left_colors = sorted(set(left))\n        right_colors = sorted(set(right))\n        \n        if len(left_colors) == 1:\n            from collections import Counter\n            maj = Counter(right).most_common(1)[0][0]\n            for i in range(half):\n                result[r][half + i] = maj\n            continue\n        \n        if len(left_colors) != 2 or len(right_colors) != 2:\n            continue\n        \n        mappings = [\n            {left_colors[0]: right_colors[0], left_colors[1]: right_colors[1]},\n            {left_colors[0]: right_colors[1], left_colors[1]: right_colors[0]}\n        ]\n        \n        best_mapping = None\n        best_score = -1\n        for m in mappings:\n            score = sum(1 for i in range(half) if m[left[i]] == right[i])\n            if score > best_score:\n                best_score = score\n                best_mapping = m\n        \n        for i in range(half):\n            result[r][half + i] = best_mapping[left[i]]\n    \n    return result\n```",
  "script": "def solve(grid):\n    rows = len(grid)\n    cols = len(grid[0])\n    half = cols // 2\n    result = [row[:] for row in grid]\n    \n    for r in range(rows):\n        left = grid[r][:half]\n        right = grid[r][half:]\n        \n        left_colors = sorted(set(left))\n        right_colors = sorted(set(right))\n        \n        if len(left_colors) == 1:\n            from collections import Counter\n            maj = Counter(right).most_common(1)[0][0]\n            for i in range(half):\n                result[r][half + i] = maj\n            continue\n        \n        if len(left_colors) != 2 or len(right_colors) != 2:\n            continue\n        \n        mappings = [\n            {left_colors[0]: right_colors[0], left_colors[1]: right_colors[1]},\n            {left_colors[0]: right_colors[1], left_colors[1]: right_colors[0]}\n        ]\n        \n        best_mapping = None\n        best_score = -1\n        for m in mappings:\n            score = sum(1 for i in range(half) if m[left[i]] == right[i])\n            if score > best_score:\n                best_score = score\n                best_mapping = m\n        \n        for i in range(half):\n            result[r][half + i] = best_mapping[left[i]]\n    \n    return result",
